('d0b0661a-179f-49cd-8f7e-cff3de2aa1d4', 'Arsenal players fighting to get fit for Tottenham derby', '<p class=\"ql-align-justify\">After the international break on November 23rd Arsenal host Tottenham in a London derby. News from the training ground and the Emirates Stadium points to Gabriel Jesus being available for action and Gunner’s striker Viktor Gyokeres and winger Noni Madueke also hope to be fit in time. The news is also positive for club captain, Martin Odegaard, Kai Havertz and Gabriel Martinelli. Odegaard’s knee problem has been well managed by the club’s medical team and he is taking part in light training during the current international break. Havertz is fighting to get back but it could be another two or three weeks before he is fully fit. If anyone doubted Pep Guardiola’s commitment to Manchester City, his celebration when Jeremy Doku scored what might well be the goal of the season against Liverpool and when Nico Gonzalez made it 2-0 his response proved he is still as keen to win the league as he ever was. This was Pep’s 1000th game in charge as a manager and coach and seeing him jumping up and down, running about and hugging his assistants proved that the great man is still totally driven, focused and energetic in his 18th season at the top level of coaching. He still takes on young players, improves, and turns them into world-class talent. Doku and Phil Foden being prime examples of what he can do with raw talent.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">Of his 1,000 games he has won 716, drawn 156 and lost 128, that is an impressive 71.6 per cent win rate.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">City are serious title contenders and will battle Mikel Arteta’s Gunners right up until the last game of the season and with Liverpool falling away at an alarming rate the title race is definitely on for Guardiola.</p><p class=\"ql-align-center\"><br></p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\"><br></p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">&nbsp;</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\"><br></p><p><br></p>', '9bc86be3-6a96-4b8c-95e7-ee4c2e74a39d', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1763180839_4b25b3a3866eb894a7a4.jpg', '2025-11-15 04:27:19.566161', '2025-11-19 04:04:41.218614', NULL),
('55e4a68c-13ae-49da-99ac-c631306ed5f2', 'Life experiences pushed man into wealth through agriculture', '<p class=\"ql-align-justify\"><strong>Deep in the lush forest of Edor, Ikom Local Government Area of Cross River State lie four expansive cocoa and plantain farms. Visitors would need more than a day to traverse the entire expanse of these verdant fields.</strong></p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">One of the farms has over 72,000 cocoa trees and over 50,000 plantain stands, where harvest is almost on a weekly basis, the farm records showed.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">The young man behind these farms is Mr Felix PaulMary Ekol, who was inspired by the circumstances of life to tap the wealth in agriculture.</p><p>Armed with a master’s degree in Accounting, Mr Ekol set up the farms himself, planting cocoa trees, plantain suckers and sleeping in the open field day and night on the farm.</p><p>Today, Ekol is called “Minister of Agriculture, Local” in his community, where his farms are, and Ogoja, where he lives with his family.</p><p class=\"ql-align-center\">“In my high school days, I was supplying food items to students on campus. They saw it and said that even the minister of agriculture hadn’t done this, so I should be named Minister of Agriculture. I told them to add ‘local’ to differentiate between the real minister of agriculture and the cloned one, which is myself,” he said.Growing up in a farming community helped him, but the real challenge that pushed him further into agriculture came from the rough experiences of life.</p><p>“While growing up as a child, we practised farming, but the moment I gained admission into the university in 2010, life became so hard and difficult because of financial requirements in the school system – JAMB registration, clearance and all of that. I looked at it and said I really needed to assist my parents, who were peasant farmers, to advance whatever they were doing, so I picked a special interest in agriculture, such that I could go from class to class educating the students, giving them orientation courses in their spare time and all of that. That passion kept growing over the years. Today, 2025 marks our 15 years of real-time service and engagement in agriculture,” he said.</p><p>In an interview with the Weekend Trust on his farms, Ekol said the story of his beginning is a tale of resilience, hope, passion and determination, which spurred him, within 15 years to set up farms that produce about 1,000 tonnes of cocoa and several tons of plantains, in addition to other multiple crops.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">Recounting his childhood experience and the struggle to raise money to finance his education and the hopelessness of getting a job or empowerment after NYSC, the young man decided to return to the farm to do something different, working on the farm up to midnight with his late younger brother.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">“Today, I have a capacity of harvesting, within the existing four farms, not less than 1,000 tonnes of cocoa per harvest. Plantain should give me a truck weekly from the farm, meaning that I harvest four trucks in a month,” he said.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">Although the young farmer did not study agriculture or agriculture-related fields, he has helped many people set up their plantain and cocoa farms in many places.</p><p class=\"ql-align-justify\">“I studied accounting, and I was doing it as one of those frustrating things because I didn’t have anywhere to lay my hands on. Let me just do it to sustain myself through that space so that hopefully, when I graduate I can gain a good job in government parastatals, agencies or whatever. However, after we graduated and the real mirage of being a graduate dawned on us after several certification trainings and all of that, nothing was forthcoming, to a point where I was frustrated. So I said to myself that if I had gone back to the little farming I was doing while going to school, I would advance it. That was when I brought in agricultural skills and meshed them with accounting knowledge and named it ‘Biological Assets Farming,’” he explained.</p><p><br></p><p class=\"ql-align-center\"><br></p><p class=\"ql-align-center\"><br></p>', '147b4f6c-5a01-48d0-b45e-925d23f0612b', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1763181057_d4d80c22e6242deebb61.jpg', '2025-11-15 04:30:57.095455', '2025-11-19 04:04:37.462017', NULL),
